into semisimplicial $k$-modules $\mathrm{Lie}(X_*)$ , thus we should get, besides
abelianization, another remarkable functor\hand{,} from $\hand{\mathcal{M}_1}(k)$ \inserted{say} to $D.(Ab_k)$ :
\[ (8) \qquad \mathrm{Lie}: \hand{\mathcal{M}_1}(k) \longrightarrow D.(Ab_k) \ \inserted{via}, \ X_* \longmapsto \mathrm{Lie}(X_*) \ , \]
granting that $X_* \mapsto \mathrm{Lie}(X_*)$ transforms quasiisomorphisms into quasiiso-%
morphisms. Now that this must be so follows from the really startling
formula
\[ (9) \qquad \hand{\pi}_i(X_*) \hand{\simeq} \hand{\pi}_i(\mathrm{Lie}(X_*)) \quad (\hand{\simeq} H_i \text{ of the associated chain} \]
\[ \text{complex in } Ab_k \ ) \ , \]
where the left hand side, I recall, is defined as
\[ \hand{(10)} \qquad \hand{\pi}_i(X_*) = \hand{\pi}_i(X_*(k)) \ . \]

