\[ \hand{(1)} \qquad f\colon (A,\underline{O}_A)\longrightarrow (B,\underline{O}_B) \quad , \]
it does'nt seem that formation of $f_!$ commutes to restriction of
rings of operators (to the constant rings $\underline{Z}_A$ and $\underline{Z}_B$ say), namely that
for a given $\underline{O}_A$-module $F$, $f_!(A)$ may be interpreted as just $f^{ab}_{o!}(F)$ with
suitable \unsure{operatiions} of $\underline{O}_B$ on the latter, where
\[ \hand{(2)} \qquad f_o : A\longrightarrow B \]
is the map in (Cat) underlying $f$. When reducing to a \inserted{suitable} ``universal'' case,
this may be expressed by saying that for given map $f_o$ and given abelian
sheaf $F$ on $A$, if we define
\[ \margin{\deleted{\illegible{1}}} \qquad \over{\underline{O}}{\underline{O}}_A= \underline{End}_{\underline{Z}}(F) \quad , \quad \underline{O}_B = f_*(\underline{O}_A) \ , \]
there does'nt seem to be a natural operation of $\underline{O}_B$ upon $f^{\hand{ab}}_{\hand{o!}}(F)$; all we
can say, it seems, is that the ring of global sections of $\underline{O}_B$ operates
o\over{n}{n} $f^{ab}_{\hand{o!}}(F)$ . More generally, reverting to the general case of a map $f$
of ringed objects in (Cat), the ring of global sections of $\underline{O}_B$ operates
on $f^{ab}_{o!}(F)$. When $\underline{O}_B$ is a constant sheaf of rings $k_B$ , this implies
of course that $k$ operates on this abelian sheaf on $B$, from which will
follow by an obvious argument that with this structure of a sheaf of
$k$-modules, $f^{ab}_{o!}(F)$ may indeed be identified with $f_!(F)$.

